# Aesthetic Clinic Market Intelligence

**A production-grade data pipeline that turns scattered industry noise into a queryable, continuously-updating source of truth — built and actively maintained by a solo developer.**

> **Status: actively under construction.** This is not a finished product and not a weekend project. The ingestion pipeline below is live, running on a schedule, and has already been audited and fixed in production. The dashboard and chat interface are in active development. See [Current status](#current-status) for exactly what's real today.

---

## The problem

"What's happening in the med spa / aesthetic clinic industry right now?" is a genuinely hard question. The answer is scattered across Reddit threads, industry news, academic papers, device manufacturer announcements, and podcast transcripts — none of it structured, none of it in one place, and most of it too noisy to just point an LLM at and hope for the best.

This project is my answer to that problem: a real ingestion pipeline, a real database, and — this is the part most "I built a RAG app" projects skip — a real answer to the question **"what happens when vector search isn't the right tool?"**

## What makes this different from a typical RAG demo

If you've seen one RAG project, you've seen the pattern: load some docs, chunk them, embed them, cosine similarity, done. This project deliberately goes further, in ways that came from hitting real problems, not from a tutorial:

- **Vector search can't answer "what's trending."** It can tell you what's *similar*, not what's *increasing*. So this pipeline maintains a separate, structured aggregation layer — topic-tagged content, rolled up over time — specifically for trend questions, with a query router that decides which path a question actually needs.
- **A trained classifier instead of an LLM call, on purpose.** Every ingested item needs a topic label, forever, at scale. Rather than pay an LLM API call per item forever, this project bootstraps labels once with an LLM, then trains a lightweight classifier **on embeddings the pipeline already computed for retrieval** — reusing an existing asset instead of building a new one. Near-zero marginal cost per item, no ongoing API spend.
- **Context-aware ingestion, not naive chunking.** A three-word Reddit reply like *"Hahahahaha"* is meaningless in isolation. The pipeline injects parent-post (and, when relevant, parent-comment) context at ingestion time, so both the embeddings and the topic classifier actually have something to work with — a problem most tutorial-grade scrapers never notice, let alone fix.
- **Self-auditing, because I found real bugs by auditing it myself.** This pipeline has already caught and fixed a silent 6-day ingestion gap, a mislabeled "1,700 duplicate records" false alarm that turned out to be a bad query (not bad data), and a chunking model that was correctly returning garbage on empty-body content. Every fix here came from actually interrogating the data, not assuming it was fine.

## Current status

**Live and running:**
- Multi-source scraping across 6 independent sources: Reddit posts, Reddit comments, industry news, academic papers (MDPI), device-manufacturer news, and podcast transcripts
- Scheduled ingestion via GitHub Actions, running headed browser automation (Playwright + xvfb) on a daily cron
- A unified content layer (`content_items`) with source-agnostic metadata (title, url, author, timestamp) so retrieval doesn't need source-specific branching
- Sync-state tracking (`is_normalised`) so the pipeline always knows exactly what's been processed and what hasn't — no silent staleness
- Chunked embedding pipeline (`sentence-transformers`, local, free) with parent-context injection for short-form content
- Full referential-integrity auditing: zero orphaned embeddings, zero un-synced backlog, verified against the live database, not assumed

**In active development:**
- Fixed taxonomy + bootstrapped topic classification (LLM-labeled sample → logistic regression trained on existing embeddings)
- `topic_rollups` table for time-windowed trend aggregation
- Two-path chat query router: structured aggregation for trend questions, vector search for semantic ones
- A dashboard surfacing volume trends, spikes, and recent activity

**Planned:**
- A self-auditing pipeline-health agent — the same audit process I've been running manually gets automated: check sync freshness, flag anomalies, escalate only when something's actually wrong
- Engagement-velocity tracking for Reddit content (a second, separately-scheduled re-check job, not bolted onto the discovery scraper)

## Architecture

```
Raw sources (6, independent)
        │
        ▼
Scheduled scraper (GitHub Actions, daily)
        │
        ▼
Normalize (context injection, dedup via unique constraint)
        │
        ▼
content_items  ──────────────┬──────────────
   (unified,                 │
   source-agnostic            ▼
   metadata)          Embeddings (pgvector)      Topic classifier
        │                     │                  (reused embeddings)
        │                     ▼                          │
        │              Semantic search                   ▼
        │                     │              content_item_topics → topic_rollups
        │                     │                          │
        └─────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┘
                              ▼
                  Query router (trend vs semantic)
                    │                    │
              Dashboard              Chat interface
```

## Tech stack

**Data & pipeline:** Python, Playwright, PostgreSQL (Supabase), pgvector
**ML:** sentence-transformers (`all-MiniLM-L6-v2`), scikit-learn
**Automation:** GitHub Actions (scheduled, headed-browser via xvfb)
**Planned:** FastAPI, a lightweight LLM-based query router

## Engineering decisions worth knowing about

A few choices here were deliberate trade-offs, not defaults — happy to talk through the reasoning on any of them:

- **Classical ML (logistic regression) over a neural network for topic classification** — with a few hundred bootstrapped labels and already-informative embedding features, a simple model generalizes better and is trivially fast to retrain than a model that needs more data to justify its complexity.
- **A many-to-many topic table, not one label per item** — real content often spans categories (a device complaint is both a device post *and* a complaint), and forcing a single label throws that information away.
- **Structured aggregation as a first-class path, not a vector-search workaround** — trend questions and semantic questions are different questions and deserve different query paths, not one tool stretched to cover both.

## Why this exists

I'm building this as part of becoming an AI systems engineer — not to have a resume bullet that says "built a RAG app," but because the real, unglamorous engineering problems (stale syncs, bad taxonomies, context-free chunking, cost-vs-latency tradeoffs) are the actual job, and I wanted to have really hit them, not just read about them.

This README will keep changing as the project does. If you're reading this and something above is now out of date, that's a feature of an actively maintained project, not a bug in the documentation.